 "context": "<figure id=\"fig-ch22_04_05\"><span data-type=\"media\" id=\"fs-idm46000\" data-alt=\"The micrograph shows clusters of round bacteria clinging to a surface. Each bacterium is about 0.4 microns across.\">\n<img src=\"https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20241024.164013/resources/cd3faef36d404cb992ba663c6fa6d99238bc4e6e\" data-media-type=\"image/jpg\" alt=\"The micrograph shows clusters of round bacteria clinging to a surface. Each bacterium is about 0.4 microns across.\" width=\"295\" height=\"200\">\n</span>\n\n</figure>",
 "stimulus_html": "",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 520302,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "<figure class=\"unnumbered\">\n<img src=\"exercises/media/cadc094c19035386.jpg\" alt=\"\">\n<figcaption>(credit: modification of work by Michael W Peck/ResearchGate)</figcaption>\n</figure>",
   "stem_html": "<p>Botulism is a potentially fatal food-borne disease. It is caused by toxins from the bacteria <i>Clostridium botulinum</i> (<i>C. botulinum</i>). This bacteria produces spores, which are difficult to destroy. The graph shows how heating affects <i>C. botulinum</i> spores. The spores are heated to 75°C and kept at that temperature.</p>\n<p>Make a claim based on this graph.</p>",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1016659,
     "content_html": "Heating to 75°C kill <i>C. botulinum</i> spores almost instantly."
    },
    {
     "id": 1016660,
     "content_html": "Keeping the spores at 75°C for 10 minutes kills most of the spores."
    },
    {
     "id": 1016661,
     "content_html": "For most spores to die, the 75°C temperature must be kept for more than two hours."
    },
    {
     "id": 1016662,
     "content_html": "Heating to 75°C has very little effect on <i>C. botulinum</i> spores."
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
